---
<Layout title="Convolution">
    <div slot="navtree">
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush py-0">
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Why'>Why Is Convolution Important?</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#What'>What Is Convolution?</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Properties'>Properties of Convolution</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Example'>Example Problem</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    
    <Section title="Convolution"/>
    <SubSection title="Why Is Convolution Important?" id="Why">
      <p>Imagine you are a doctor and to fight an infection you must give your patients 3 doses of medicine. </p>
        <p>Doses = 3</p>
        <p>At the beginning of the week, you have 1 patient. This number increases throughout the week by 1 patient each day. By Friday, you have 5 patients. </p>
        <p>Number of patients per day = [1 2 3 4 5]</p>
        <p>If you wanted to calculate the total number of doses you administer over the week, you would multiply the doses by the number of patients per day and sum this.</p>
        <p>Total doses = [3 6 9 12 15] = 45</p>
        <p>However, imagine the disease evolves. Now, you have to give each patient 3 doses on the first day, 2 doses on the second day, and 1 dose on the third day.</p>
        <p>Doses = [3 2 1]</p>
        <p>Now, how many total doses are administered each day? Each week? This suddenly became more complicated, but convolution is the key to solving this problem.</p>
    </SubSection>
    
    <SubSection title="What Is Convolution?" id="What">
      <p>This is a way to take two functions and apply them to each other. You take the first function and map the second function to it. Simplistically, you are taking both functions and performing a sliding multiplication and sum of them.</p>
      <p>Often, we do not know the output of a system, <InlineEquation equation="y(t)"/>, but we do know the input, <InlineEquation equation="x(t)"/>, and the impulse response, <InlineEquation equation="h(t)"/>. The convolution operation allows us to use <InlineEquation equation="x(t)"/> and <InlineEquation equation="h(t)"/> to solve for <InlineEquation equation="y(t)"/>:</p>
      <DisplayEquation equation="y(t) = x(t) * h(t)"/>
      <p><span class="fw-bold">Important:</span> The asterisk (<InlineEquation equation="*"/>) is the symbol used for convolution; it does not mean standard multiplication. In continuous time, this is expressed analytically with an integral:</p>
      <DisplayEquation equation="y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)d\\tau"/>
    </SubSection>
    
    <SubSection title="Properties of Convolution" id="Properties">
        <p>The commutative property:</p><DisplayEquation equation="x_{1}(t)*x_{2}(t) = x_{2}(t)*x_{1}(t)"/>
        <p>The distributive property:</p> <DisplayEquation equation="x_{1}(t)*[x_{2}(t)+x_{3}(t)] = x_{1}(t)*x_{2}(t)+x_{1}(t)*x_{3}(t)"/>
        <p>The associative property:</p><DisplayEquation equation="x_{1}(t)*[x_{2}(t)*x_{3}(t)] = [x_{1}(t)*x_{2}(t)]*x_{3}(t)"/>
    </SubSection>
    
    <SubSection title="Example Problem" id="Example">
      <Example id="doctor_doses" title="Medication Dosing via Discrete Convolution" solution="True">
        <div>
          <p>Returning to the scenario described earlier:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Patients per day:</strong> Increases from 1 on Monday to 5 on Friday &rarr; <InlineEquation equation="x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]"/></li>
            <li><strong>Dosing regimen:</strong> 3 doses on day 1, 2 doses on day 2, 1 dose on day 3 &rarr; <InlineEquation equation="h = [3, 2, 1]"/></li>
          </ul>
          <p>Determine the total number of doses administered each day and over the entire week using discrete convolution (<InlineEquation equation="y = x * h"/>).</p>
        </div>

        <div slot="solution">
          <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Reverse ("flip") the patient array: <InlineEquation equation="[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]"/>.</p>
          <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Slide the dosing array <InlineEquation equation="[3, 2, 1]"/> across the reversed patient array from right to left, multiplying aligned pairs and summing the products for each day:</p>

          <div class="my-3">
            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 1:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
            [3 2 1]
[5 4 3 2 1]
Product: 3 &times; 1 = 3 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 2:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
         [3 2 1]
[5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 2) + (2 &times; 1) = 8 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 3:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
      [3 2 1]
[5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 3) + (2 &times; 2) + (1 &times; 1) = 14 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 4:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
   [3 2 1]
[5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 4) + (2 &times; 3) + (1 &times; 2) = 20 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 5:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
[3 2 1]
[5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 5) + (2 &times; 4) + (1 &times; 3) = 26 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 6:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
[3 2 1]
   [5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 0) + (2 &times; 5) + (1 &times; 4) = 14 doses</pre>

            <p class="mb-1"><strong>Day 7:</strong></p>
<pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded text-dark font-monospace mb-2">
[3 2 1]
      [5 4 3 2 1]
Product: (3 &times; 0) + (2 &times; 0) + (1 &times; 5) = 5 doses</pre>
          </div>

          <p><strong>Resulting Doses Array:</strong></p>
          <DisplayEquation equation="y = [3, 8, 14, 20, 26, 14, 5]"/>
          <p>Summing all daily doses, the total number of doses administered across all 7 days is <strong>90 doses</strong>.</p>
        </div>
      </Example>
    </SubSection>
</Layout>
